label weeks
[signal arg="bg:infirmary"]
He was out for three weeks. Doc explained it later, in the flat, unhurried way he explained everything.
The blade had gone in close enough to the spine that any healer without exceptional control would have killed him trying to close it too fast.
What Doc used, in the end, was a slow, careful Regeneration Shir, coaxing torn tissue back into itself thread by thread over days rather than minutes.
It was the exact discipline Liesel had spent two years quietly teaching herself out of borrowed texts,
and had never been allowed to practice on a person.
Kliam sat in a cell beneath the Order of Knights' headquarters for all three of those weeks, saying nothing. Aftgan had not been found.
Lan knew none of this while it was happening. Just a long gray absence that ended, finally, with light.
label first_faces
Liesel (Flat): You're awake.
Her voice cracked on the second word in a way he'd never heard from her.
Lan (Acting): Apparently. How long?
Liesel (Default): Three weeks. Don't ever do that again. I mean it.
Liesel (Default): I have spent three weeks reading everything this academy owns about spinal trauma and I never want to need any of it again.
Florentine (Flat): You almost died protecting an event where I was the one who nearly got kidnapped. Do you understand how that makes me feel?
Lan (Smug): Grateful?
Florentine (Mad): Guilty. But also grateful. Don't let it go to your head.
set {aff_florentine} += 1
label enriko
Enriko: You scared the absolute life out of me.
Enriko: I ran the entire way from the lower district and I still didn't make it in time, and I have been furious about that every day since.
Lan (Default): You made it in time for the part that mattered. Richard and Ingrid showing up wasn't luck.
Enriko: That was me, actually.
Enriko: When I realized I wasn't going to make the green on foot, I diverted to the Order of Knights' nearest post and told them everything.
Enriko: Kliam, the target, the timing. Richard and Ingrid were the two closest to combat-ready.
Enriko: I got lucky they were the ones who happened to be there.
Lan (Smile): You saved my life from three streets away. That's not luck. That's just you.
Enriko didn't have a joke ready for that, which told Lan more than any answer would have.
set {aff_enriko} += 2
label veldero
Veldero: I couldn't stop it. The stab. I saw it coming a half-second too late and I've replayed that half-second more times than I want to admit.
- "You kept me alive for four minutes."
	set {aff_veldero} += 1
	Lan (Default): You kept me alive for four minutes against a man who could've ended both of us in one. I don't blame you for the half-second you didn't have.
	Veldero: I know that logically. Working on believing it.
- "What did you see?"
	Lan (Sus): What did you see? Right before.
	Veldero: Nothing. That's the problem. There was nothing there. It just[pause=0.35][lspeed=0.25!]...[lspeed] appeared.
label ilia
Dialog/Ilia (Def): I'm not going to make a joke.
Dialog/Ilia (Def): I thought about six different ones on the walk over and none of them felt right,
Dialog/Ilia (Def): which has genuinely never happened to me, so consider this a historic occasion.
Dialog/Ilia (Happy): You gave me a real scare, sixty-seven. I don't sit up nights worrying about most of the people I spar with. Heal up.
Dialog/Ilia (Happy): I've got a rematch to collect on eventually, and it's no fun beating someone who's still recovering.
set {aff_ilia} += 2
label leonora
Leonora came the next morning, alone, and closed the door behind her with a quiet deliberateness that told Lan this wasn't a social visit.
Leonora (Default): I know who authorized the gap in the forest patrol. I've known for four days.
Leonora (Default): I didn't come sooner because I needed to be certain, and because once I said it aloud I couldn't unsay it.
Leonora (Mad): My brother. Edric.
Leonora (Mad): Second in line, which he's spent his life resenting, because second in line means nothing if the first keeps breathing and doing her duty competently.
Leonora (Mad): He arranged the ambush in the forest. Both prongs, mine and Raiz's.
Leonora (Mad): Meant to look like each country struck the other first.
Leonora (Mad): Meant, I think, to make me look weak enough, or dead enough, that the succession answered itself.
Leonora (Default): I don't have proof that would hold up publicly.
Leonora (Default): I have proof that satisfies me completely, which is a different and more dangerous thing to carry around.
Leonora (Default): I asked you to tell me anything that kept me alive, the moment you could. You never did. I've wondered whether you already knew.
- "I suspected. I had no proof."
	set {aff_leonora} += 1
	Lan (Default): I suspected. I didn't have proof either.
- "I had a bad feeling. Nothing more."
	set {aff_leonora} += 1
	Lan (Acting): A bad feeling. Nothing I could have handed you.
Lan (Default): I still don't have anything I could have handed you that would have helped more than waiting.
It wasn't the whole truth. It was closer than anything he'd given her before.
Leonora (Default): When I have proof, you'll be the first I tell. Not because I trust you completely.
Leonora (Default): Because you've earned that much, and I intend to keep exact accounts.
Leonora (Smile): Rest. You look terrible. I mean it kindly.
label raiz_note
A folded note lay on the bedside table, left with the infirmary orderly. Neat, blunt handwriting.
Raiz: Schwert. Han was fast and stupid. Kliam is slow and clever.
Raiz: You lost to the second because you tried to beat him with the first's plan. Learn the difference. Al Khalid.
if {aff_raiz} >= 2:
	A second line was written below, smaller. "If you want to argue the point, I will listen."
	set {aff_raiz} += 1
else:
	Lan (Sus): (Rank one writes to rank whatever I am. I didn't expect that.)
label amir
Amir came the following afternoon, and Lan hadn't expected him at all. He didn't sit. He stood at the foot of the bed with his arms crossed.
Amir: You've had a hard few months. Hiding what you are.
Amir: Getting found out anyway, piece by piece, by people who kept your secret instead of using it.
Amir: Nearly dying for a girl who isn't even in your department, against a man three tiers above anything this academy trains its first-years to survive.
Amir: I know what that weighs like, boy. I've carried worse versions of it myself.
Lan (Default): I'm managing.
Amir: You're surviving. Those aren't the same thing.
Amir: I want to make you an offer. My assistant.
Amir: Officially, on the record, so nobody asks awkward questions about why a first-year keeps disappearing into my training hall after hours.
Amir: I'll teach you properly. Full use of your gi, not the careful trickle you've been rationing.
Amir: And it's time somebody started you on shir as well. You've got the discipline.
Amir: You've simply never had anyone willing to spend the time.
- "I need a few days."
	Lan (Default): Give me some time. I just woke up.
- "Let me think about it."
	Lan (Smug): Let me think about it. I'm still mostly bandages.
Amir: The offer doesn't come with an expiration. When you're ready, my door's open.
Amir: Berthram already knows I'm asking. He approves. Make of that whatever you like.
Then he was gone, and Lan lay back against the pillows with a great deal more to think about than the ache still sitting deep in his spine,
and, somewhere under it, the first real flicker of something that might, eventually, become hope.
[signal arg="chapter_end:ch14"]
[end_timeline]
